## A B S T R A C T
Keywords: Natural polysaccharide Metal-organic-frameworks Synergistic antibacterial Water disinfection
Pathogens transmitted through the water environment pose a great threat to human health. Hence developing more reliable and efficient antibacterial materials to eliminate bacterium in water environments is urgent. Herein, we posed a novel strategy of interweaving carboxymethyl chitosan (CMCS) and $\mathtt{A g/C u}$ -MOFs to construct renewable Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS composite beads with rapid sterilization, long-term antibacterial effects and high biosafety. Characterizations revealed that CMCS and bimetallic MOFs act as the “warehouses” of metal ions and played key roles in anchoring, storage, delivery, and controlled release of metal ions. The synergistic antibac­ terial effect achieved by the combination of ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ provides the composite beads with high antibacterial efficiency, resulting in low minimum inhibitory concentrations $(0.32~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ against E. coli and $0.16{\mathrm{~mg/mL}}$ against S. aureus) and over $99.9~\%$ bacteria killing rate. Benefiting from the rapid release of metal ions from polymer chains and the long-term release from MOFs, the composite beads can effectively sterilize the simulated swimming pool water in $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ and persistently inhibit bacterial reproduction over $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and show a safe level of residual heavy metals because of the chelation of CMCS. This work provides new insights and promises a strategy for the design and commercial application of novel water fungicides.
## 1. Introduction
Many diseases can be spread through the water environment, such as typhoid fever, hepatitis, and tuberculosis, which cause enormous harm to human health (Burch & Thomas, 1998; Kumar et al., 2021). Over the past century, the use of antibiotics is the main way to treat bacterial infection (Shao et al., 2018). However, the problem of bacterial drug resistance has become more and more severe (Huh & Kwon, 2011; Pang et al., 2019). Therefore, developing reliable and efficient antibacterial materials to directly eliminate bacteria in water environments may be a better means of protecting humans from infectious diseases than developing new drugs.
At present, the use of chlorine-containing disinfectants is still the most common sterilization method for urban water, especially for swimming pool water. Such chemicals generally have problems con­ taining pungent odor, irritation of the skin and mucous membranes, carcinogenic risk of by-products, and poor sustained antibacterial effect (Shao et al., 2018). In addition, although the disinfection efficiency of these drugs is enough, their decomposition is easy, resulting in a short duration of bacteriostasis. Especially for circulating water, such as swimming pool water, high frequency and excessive feeding caused the risk of halogenated disinfection by-product accumulation (Heaney et al., 2020; Peng et al., 2016). Other alternative methods, such as ozone and ultraviolet irradiation, also have drawbacks like toxic by-products and short duration of effect (W. J. Huang et al., 2005).
Chitosan and other natural polysaccharides have the advantages such as wide sources and good biocompatibility (Pooresmaeil et al., 2021; Salama et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2022). But their antibacterial properties are unsatisfactory. The general strategy is combining natural polysaccharides with antibacterial active substances, such as metal nanoparticles (Irshad et al., 2020), metal ions (Liu et al., 2017a; Qin et al., 2007), and quaternary ammonium salt (Chen et al., 2021), to prepare composite materials for the application. Among them, the ma­ terials containing metal ions have been widely used in medicine, health, and other fields due to their excellent antibacterial properties and relatively low prices (Hamad et al., 2020; Wu et al., 2014). But most of these materials have the problem of uncontrollable ions release (Karel et al., 2015). Therefore, there are often risks such as unsustainable ef­ fects and heavy metal pollution when they are used, which restricts their application in water disinfection. Meanwhile, one single type of metal ion also has its defects when used for antibacterial. For example, silver ions are easily converted into elemental silver under certain conditions, resulting in weakened antibacterial properties and blackened material color; the antibacterial efficiency of zinc, copper and other ions is un­ satisfactory. The joint use of two different metal ions is a feasible strategy to strengthen the antibacterial properties (Jin et al., 2014; Mao et al., 2019), but persistence is still a difficult problem to resolve.
MOFs are a new class of versatile precursors with unique advantages such as high porosity, large specific surface area, and easy functionali­ zation (S. Z. Wang et al., 2019; Y. Wang et al., 2020; H. C. Zhou & Kitagawa, 2014). Furthermore, MOFs are also regarded as excellent metal ion carriers, which can be used as antibacterial materials (CelisArias et al., 2018). Some kinds of MOFs have appropriate water stability and thus can release metal ions gradually with the slow disintegration of their framework structures in a water environment. This provides ideas for the preparation of novel sustained-release bactericidal agents. In addition, researchers often compound two or more metal ions to prepare antibacterial materials to solve the drawbacks of one single type of metal, while MOFs can easily support multiple metal ions by postsynthetic exchange method (PSE) without changing their framework structure and characteristics (Karmakar et al., 2021; Kim et al., 2012; Stubbs et al., 2018; S. S. Zhou et al., 2021).
Recently, many studies on polysaccharide/MOFs composites were reported (Guohuan Huang et al., 2021; Li et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2019). Nevertheless, as far as we know, there are no reports on the preparation of bimetallic MOFs@polysaccharide composites and the investigation of their antibacterial application potential. In our previous works, based on the chemical cross-linking of MOFs with the active groups of carbox­ ymethyl chitosan (CMCS), monometallic MOFs@CMCS were prepared via in-situ growth (G. Huang et al., 2020). But the antibacterial perfor­ mances of the monometallic MOFs/polysaccharide composites did not meet the requirements of water disinfection, hence we expect that the silver/copper bimetallic MOFs@CMCS obtained by the PSE method will show excellent antibacterial performance and application prospects. We have the following hypothesis: 1) During the in-situ growth process, part of the metal ions bound to CMCS acts as anchoring sites for MOFs, and the other part can still be released into the aqueous environment for sterilization. 2) After the PSE process, silver and copper ions are distributed both on CMCS and MOFs crystals, resulting in that CMCS and bimetallic MOFs in the composite can be regarded as the “warehouses” of bimetallic ions; 3) The differences in amount and duration of metal ions released from the two “warehouses” can complement the antibac­ terial properties of materials; 4) The synergistic antibacterial effect of bimetals can greatly improve the antibacterial efficiency of the bime­ tallic composites, and the anchoring of CMCS to metal ions limits the concentration of metal ions released, thereby improving the biological safety. 5) After releasing metal ions, the bimetallic MOFs@CMCS can be recycled by replenishing the metal ions to CMCS.
In this study, novel bimetallic Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS composite beads were developed by the in-situ growth of Cu-MOFs on CMCS and the cation PSE method, then characterized in detail. The metal ion release properties of the material were studied by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES), to explain the source of the excellent performance of rapid sterilization and continuous bacterio­ stasis. Regeneration and antibacterial experiments were carried out to prove its application potential. In addition, the cytotoxicity test confirmed that the material has good cellular compatibility, hence the application potential of the material was verified by taking the swim­ ming pool water as the treatment object. Overall, we creatively built a kind of ingenious metal ion “warehouses” based on the hybridization of CMCS with bimetallic MOFs with excellent antibacterial properties and high safety through PSE strategy. This material is hopeful to be used in
the field of water treatment.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Carboxymethyl chitosan (degree of substitution $280\ \%$ , $\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{W}}\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathbf{\Psi}$ 100,000–200,000 Da), trichloroisocyanuric acid $(\geq97~\%)$ , and 1,3,5- benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{{R_{3}B T C}}_{\mathrm{{\Omega}}}$ 98 %) were purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Copper(II) nitrate trihydrate and absolute ethanol $(\geq99.7~\%)$ were purchased from Chengdu Kelong Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Chengdu, China). Silver (I) nitrate was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Sodium hypochlorite $(\geq99~\%)$ was purchased from Guangdong Yunxing Biotechnology Co., Ltd. All chemicals were analytically pure and used as received without further purification.
## 2.2. Characterization
## 2.3. Fabrication of materials
## 2.3.1. Preparation of Cu-BTC
Typically, dissolve $0.5797\mathrm{g}$ (2.4 mmol) $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 0.3362 $\mathbf{g}\left(1.6\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ in $160\mathrm{mL}$ mixed solvent $(N,N-$ -Dimethylformamide: ethanol $\mathbf{\lambda}=3{:}1$ , v/v), and then reflux for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with magnetic stirring. Wash the precipitates several times with ethanol and distilled water and then dry them in a vacuum for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.3.2. Preparation of $C u@C M C S$ and Ag/Cu@CMCS
Cu-BTC@CMCS was prepared according to the previously reported method (G. Huang et al., 2020). CMCS solution ( $25\mathrm{mL},4\%,w/\mathrm{w})$ was dropwise injected into a $250~\mathrm{mL}$ beaker containing $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of copper nitrate aqueous solution $\mathrm{(50~mg/mL)}$ ) and the ball-like $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{CMCS}$ were formed within a few seconds. Then the polymer matrices were washed with distilled water until no Cu (II) ions remained in the filtrate. $\mathtt{A g/}$ $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{CMCS}$ was prepared under a similar method with one change about dissolving $_{0.5~g}$ silver (I) nitrate in the copper nitrate aqueous solution before injecting the CMCS solution.
## 2.3.3. Preparation of Cu-BTC@CMCS
Cu-BTC@CMCS was prepared according to our previously reported method with some modifications (G. Huang et al., 2020). CuBTC@CMCS composite was obtained by reaction of $20\textbf{g}$ undried $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{CMCS}$ and $2\mathrm{~g~H_{3}B T C}$ in $100~\mathrm{\mL}$ mixed solvent $(N,N$ -Dime­ thylformamide: ethanol $\iota=3{:}7$ , $\nu/\mathbf{v}.$ ) for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ refluxing. The product was washed three times with ethanol to remove the unreacted $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTC}$ and then dried in a vacuum for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
## 2.3.4. Preparation of Ag/Cu-BTC and Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS
The bimetallic MOF Ag/Cu-BTC was prepared according to the pre­ viously reported method with some modifications (Gao et al., 2020). Typically, $\boldsymbol{0.02g\ A g N O_{3}}$ and $_{0.1\mathrm{~g~}}$ Cu-BTC were dissolved and dispersed into a $30~\mathrm{mL}$ mixed solution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ethanol $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{ethanol}=1{:}1,\nu/$ v). After ultrasonic treatment for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ , the solution was put in a stainless-steel autoclave equipped with a polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) liner and heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ in an oven. Then, the reactor underwent a natural cooling process until room temperature. The cyan product was cleaned with ethanol and deionized water three times and dried in a $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ vacuum oven for 5 h. Ag/Cu-BTC $@$ CMCS were prepared by the same process.
## 2.4. Antibacterial test
To evaluate the antibacterial performance of materials, Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus aureus were selected as model bacteria. The samples were dispersed in bacterial suspensions $\mathrm{\cdot10~mL}$ , $10^{7}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ . Then, the bacteria/composites' suspensions were incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{{h}}}$ . Time-depended optical density at $600\mathrm{nm}$ (OD600) was measured by UV–vis spectroscopy every $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to investigate the process of bacterial reproduction.
The rapid antibacterial performances for the samples were studied via agar plate counting. The samples $(0.48~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ were dispersed in bacterial suspensions as described above. After 2-h incubation, the bacterial suspensions were diluted 100 times and cultured on solid Luria Bertani (LB) agar plates for $24\mathrm{h}$ to count the bacterial colonies.
To observe the morphologies of the bacteria, the bacteria/compos­ ite's suspensions were fixed with $2.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ glutaraldehyde solution and dehydrated with gradient ethanol solutions (25, 50, 70, 80, 90, 95, 100 $\mathrm{wt\%}$ . Then the suspensions were dropped and detected by SEM.
The minimal inhibition concentrations (MICs) of the samples were monitored. Samples at different concentrations (0.04, 0.08, 0.12, 0.16, 0.32, 0.64, and $0.96~\mathrm{mg/mL)}$ were added to the same amount of bac­ terial suspensions $(10^{7}\mathrm{CFU/mL})$ ). Then, the suspensions were incubated overnight. The concentration of the bacteria was measured by UV–vis spectroscopy at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
The inhibition zones of the samples were evaluated. Place $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of each sample in an open-wells LB agar plate $\mathrm{7}\mathrm{mm}$ diameter), spin-coat $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ $\mathrm{(10^{7}C F U/m L)}$ of bacterial solution in LB medium, then incubate at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , finally, the inhibition zones were measured.
## 2.5. Regeneration of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS
After antibacterial, Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS beads were recovered and dried. Then Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS were immersed in an $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}+\mathbf{\partial}$ and $\mathtt{C u2+}$ solution to supplement metal ions. Typically, dissolve $0.03\mathrm{g}\mathrm{AgNO3}$ and $0.1\mathrm{\g}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO3})$ 2⋅3H2O in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water, then add $0.2\mathrm{~g~Ag/Cu-}$
BTC@CMCS beads after use and stirring for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The product was cleaned with deionized water and dried in a $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ vacuum oven for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ .
## 2.6. Cytotoxicity test
The cell toxicity of the composites investigated by MTT assay (Ali­ pour & Namazi, 2020). Take HeLa cells in the logarithmic growth period to prepare $3\times10^{4}/\mathrm{ml}$ cell suspensions, then put them into a 96-well culture plate, and culture for 24-36 h. Add the composite materials suspensions of different concentrations respectively, and cultivate together for $24\mathrm{h}$ . After that, discard the supernatant, add $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ 3-(4,5- dimethyl-2-thiazolyl)-2,5-diphenyl tetrazolium bromide (MTT) solution $\mathbf{(5{\mg/mL})}$ into each well and incubate at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ . Then cen­ trifugate and discard the supernatant, add $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ dimethyl sulfoxide to each well, shake for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , measure the optical density at the wave­ length of $570\mathrm{nm}$ and calculate the survival rate of HeLa cells.
## 2.7. Study of swimming pool water disinfection
The pool was a plastic vessel containing $^4\mathrm{L}$ of water. The pool was designed to have a recycle flow rate of $50~\mathrm{mL/min}$ through a pump. At the beginning and the 24th hour in a cycle of the experiment, $400\upmu\mathrm{L}E$ . coli fluid $(1\times10^{7}\mathrm{CFU/mL})$ was added to the water. Bather insult was added every time the reactor was inoculated. Bather insult was made up as described in the literature (Goeres et al., 2004). At several different points of time, the numbers of bacteria in the water were evaluated via agar plate counting. The system operated open to the atmosphere and the water temperature was maintained at $25\pm1^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Preparation and characterization of bimetallic MOFs@CMCS
As shown in Fig. 1, firstly, copper ions were anchored on the car­ boxymethyl chitosan (CMCS) polymer chains to obtain $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{CMCS}$ and then Cu-BTC@CMCS compound material was synthesized by in-situ growth of MOFs. After that, the postsynthetic metal ion exchange method was carried out to replace a part of copper ions in the material with silver ions, so as to obtain bimetallic Ag/Cu-BTC $@$ CMCS composite beads. The diameter of the beads is $2\:\mathrm{mm}\pm0.3\:\mathrm{mm}$ , and their macro shape is relatively uniform.
The morphology of the material was confirmed by SEM (Fig. 2a and b). As the basis of the composite bead, cross-linked CMCS showed a layered structure, which is conducive to the exchange between effective components of the material and the water environment. A large number of crystals with diameters of $10\upmu\mathrm{m}$ to $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ can be seen between layers and on the surface of the natural polymer matrix, and the shape of regular octahedron conforms to the typical morphology of HKUST-1 (Cu-BTC). The crystal shape had no obvious change after the exchange of silver ions (Fig. S1 shows the image of Cu-BTC@CMCS), which is consistent with the description in the literature (Gao et al., 2020). The results of energy dispersive X-Ray spectroscopy (EDX) showed that copper was evenly distributed on the surface of the material (Fig. 2c), indicating that a part of copper ions remain on the polymer chain after Cu-BTC was grown. Silver is mainly concentrated on MOF crystal, but it also has a certain distribution on the polymer matrix. The FT-IR results showed some important information (Fig. 2d). The signals of MOFs (CuBTC and Ag/Cu-BTC) at $1647\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belong to the asymmetric stretching vibration of $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ , and this vibration in Cu-BTC@CMCS and ${\sf A g/C u-}$ BTC@CMCS shifted to a lower wavenumber $(1620~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ , which is due to the cross-linking of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ ions with the carboxymethyl group (Sun & Wang, 2006). Benzene ring absorption signals appeared at 729 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ in composites, which proved the existence of ligand BTC. It is worth noting that there is a new vibration $(1008~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ in $\scriptstyle{\mathtt{A g/C u-B T C}}$ and $\mathtt{A g/C u-B T C@C M C S}$ after silver ion exchange, which could be attributed to the out-of-plane vibration of (CO) O–H bond on the ben­ zene ring (Embrechts et al., 2018; Palafox et al., 2002). Due to the replacement of divalent copper ions by monovalent silver ions, the carboxylates on a part of ligands are out of the coordination state after PSE, and could partially protonate and convert into carboxyl groups in an acidic environment, resulting in the enhancement of the vibration. These strongly proved the successful doping of silver ions in the material.
The thermal stability of the composites was analyzed by TG, as shown in Fig. 2e. Before ${150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}},$ , the mass loss of the composites is mainly caused by the bound water and crystal water contained in the materials (Sreedhar et al., 2007). At $175\mathrm{-}190^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the mass of $\mathtt{C u@C M C S}$ rapidly decreased by $550~\%$ , which attributed to the decomposition of CMCS (Hu et al., 2016; Pas¸cala˘u et al., 2012). The mass loss of Cu-BTC@CMCS and $\mathtt{A g/C u-B T C}@\mathtt{C N}$ CS in this temperature range is smaller, indicating that the introduction of MOFs can greatly improve the thermal stability of the composites. And the thermal stability of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS is better than that of Cu-BTC@CMCS. This can be attributed to the com­ bination of silver ions with the remaining coordination sites on CMCS. In addition, the content of ligand BTC in Cu-BTC@CMCS was obtained through the ash qualities of $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{CMCS}$ and Cu-BTC@CMCS, and the amount of HKUST-1 in Cu-BTC@CMCS was further calculated (38.53 wt $\%$ (Liu et al., 2017b; Zhu et al., 2016). Moreover, in the process of preparation of Cu-BTC@CMCS, a large number of copper ions on CMCS are transferred to MOFs, and the empty coordination sites then bind to silver ions in the subsequent PSE process, resulting in a significant ash mass difference between Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS and Cu-BTC@CMCS.
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of Cu-BTC, the bimetallic MOF Ag/Cu-BTC and the composites were compared (Fig. 2f). The results show that obvious feature peaks of HKUST-1 are visible on the com­ posites, confirming the successful growth of the MOF crystals. Further­ more, the characteristic diffraction peaks of HKUST-1 before and after the PSE step have no obvious change, revealing that silver ion exchange does not change the crystal structure. Fig. 2g–i shows the X-ray photo­ electron spectroscopy (XPS) of the materials. Both the survey scan spectrum and the Ag 3d high-resolution spectra confirm the presence of Ag elements in the composites. The relative intensity of the peak at 932.7 eV for Cu-BTC@CMCS and Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS in the Cu $\mathsf{2p}$ spectra is enlarged compared to that of the pure MOF, which is due to a certain amount of copper ions combined with amino groups on CMCS (G. Huang et al., 2019).
According to the above discussion, the bimetallic composites Ag/CuBTC@CMCS were successfully prepared. Meantime, we noted that in the process of synthesizing Cu-BTC@CMCS from Cu@CMCS, some copper ions will remain on CMCS chains instead of all forming MOFs. Similarly, in addition to replacing copper ions in MOFs, silver ions in the PSE process will inevitably bind to carboxyl and amino sites on the CMCS chains that are not bound by copper ions. Therefore, we expect that MOFs can provide long-term ion release through the slow disintegration of their crystal structure, while the metal-crosslinked CMCS component in the composite may be difficult to have a good long-term metal ion release capacity, but could quickly release more metal ions at the initial stage of application.
## 3.2. Metal-ion-release properties of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS
In order to compare the metal ion release properties of the materials, bimetallic cross-linked material ${\sf A g/C u@C M C S}$ was prepared by a similar method as the preparation of Cu@CMCS. The contents of silver and copper in Ag/Cu-BTC, Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS, and ${\sf A g/C u@C M C S}$ were listed in Table 1. The ratio of contents of silver and copper are similar among the three materials.
Subsequently, the metal ion release properties of Ag/Cu-BTC, Ag/CuBTC@CMCS, and ${\sf A g/C u@C M C S}$ in water within 48 h were studied. The results are shown in Fig. 3. In general, Ag/Cu-BTC has low but increasing release rates of silver and copper ions, showing relative water stability. The cumulative relative release amounts of the simple metal-crosslinked material ${\sf A g/C u@C M C S}$ were the highest. It released a large number of metal ions in the first few hours and hardly releases any more after that, which proves that the release of ions from CMCS is fast but limited.
Actually, chitosan derivatives were regarded as heavy metal adsor­ bents due to their excellent chelating abilities(Bailey et al., 1999; Kat­ sutoshi et al., 1997; Pooresmaeil & Namazi, 2020). Here we consider that during the crosslinking with ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ or the PSE process, most of the active sites on CMCS have been combined with metal ions. Hence the metal-crosslinked CMCS can release some of the metal ions in pure water and quickly achieve the adsorption equilibrium.
The amount of metal ions released from $\mathtt{A g/C u-B T C@C M C S}$ is be­ tween the above two composites, and about $7.46~\%~\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$ and $9.36~\%$ $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ can be released in $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . In addition, Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS compos­ ites can also release more ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ in $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ than $\mathtt{A g/C u}$ -BTC. The above results show that Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS can release ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ rapidly, which is mainly due to the rapid release of metal ions bound on the CMCS chain. Owing to the stability of bimetallic MOFs crystals, the effective release time of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS is also longer than two other materials. Such metal ion release characteristics offer the basis for high-efficiency bacteria killing, and make the composite the potential to provide a lasting antibacterial effect in application.
## 3.3. Antibacterial performances and renewable property
First, a simple experiment was carried out to verify the synergistic antibacterial effect of copper ions and silver ions. As shown in Fig. S2, the experimental results indicate that the antibacterial efficiency of copper ion on S. aureus is not very satisfying, so the sterilization rate is $<80\%$ when the concentration of copper ion is $20\ \mathrm{ppm}$ . Relatively, the killing rate of $98\%$ can be achieved when the concentration of silver ion is as low as $5\mathrm{ppm}$ , and the effect of complete sterilization can be ach­ ieved when the ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ concentration increase to about 8 ppm. As two kinds of metal ions were jointly used in the antibacterial experiment with low concentrations $2\ \mathrm{ppm}$ for silver ion and $10~\mathrm{ppm}$ for copper ion), the killing rate of S. aureus was $99.9\mathrm{~\}99.$ . Therefore, we preliminarily confirmed the synergistic antibacterial effect of copper ion and silver ion, that is, on the premise of achieving the same antibacterial effect, the joint use of two metal ions can significantly reduce the minimum amount of metal requirement, compared with the use of single species of metal ions. This is of great significance to reduce the threat of heavy metal.
Then the antibacterial properties of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS were sys­ tematically studied. First, after co-cultured with the composites with different dosages, the bacterial reproduction was investigated and measured by the optical density at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ wavelength. As shown in
Fig. 4a and b, the minimum inhibitory concentrations (MIC) of ${\sf A g/C u-}$ BTC@CMCS against $E$ . coli and S. aureus were 0.32 and $0.16~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ , respectively. The MIC values of pure MOF (Ag/Cu-BTC) are slightly lower because of its higher total metal loading. In contrast, CuBTC@CMCS material cannot effectively inhibit the reproduction of bacteria even at a higher dosage $(0.96~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ , showing a poor anti­ bacterial property. The change of absorbance during the culture process was monitored. After $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ , the OD600 values of control groups of E. coli and S. aureus were about 1.03 ABS and 0.57 ABS, respectively, while the OD600 values of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS group remained under 0.1 ABS within $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . This indicates that Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS can effectively inhibit bacterial reproduction with a very low dosage.
By observing the SEM image of the bacterial corpse (Fig. S3), it can be found that the surface of the untreated bacteria is relatively smooth and the structure is complete, while the bacterial membranes of the $E$ . coli and S. aureus treated with Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS are significantly wrinkled and damaged, which is consistent with the destruction of heavy metal ions (Wyszogrodzka et al., 2016). At present, the mecha­ nism of heavy metal ions sterilization contains two aspects: i) metal ions react with sulfhydryl groups in proteins, causing denaturation of bacterial proteins, and further destroying the integrity of the membrane (Feng et al., 2000); ii) metal ions can catalyze the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS), thereby causing the oxidative damage of DNA, proteins, and lipids (Park et al., 2009; Pham et al., 2013). In addition, researchers demonstrated that the joint use of ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ ion and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ ion could generate more ROS than the single-use (X. Wang et al., 2016), which may explain the synergistic antibacterial effect in this study.
The antibacterial properties of the materials before and after the PSE process were compared through the inhibition zone experiments. Fig. 4c shows that both Ag/Cu-BTC and Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS have enlarged inhibition zones against E. coli and S. aureus to some extent after exchanging silver ions, indicating that our strategy of bimetallic syner­ gistic antibacterial is effective.
The speed of killing bacteria is also an important indicator to mea­ sure the antibacterial ability of materials, so the bacterial killing rate of the composites within $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ was evaluated using agar plate counting. As shown in Fig. 4d, the number of colonies of the bacteria treated with $0.48~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS was 0, indicating that the composite can completely kill bacteria in a short time at a low concentration. In contrast, the sterilization rate of $\mathtt{C u-B T C@C M C S}$ without silver ions is $<95\%$ , because the lower antibacterial efficiency of single copper ions is not enough for killing $100\%$ of bacteria at low concentrations. Espe­ cially, $0.24~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ Ag/Cu-BTC (the metal content is about the same as $0.48\mathrm{mg/mL}$ Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS) could not kill bacteria totally within 2 h. Combined with the discussion of the ion release properties in the previous section, it can be considered that the rapid release of metal ions bound to the CMCS chains in an aqueous environment has a significant contribution to the rapid bactericidal properties of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS.
In addition, we investigated the performance of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS in long-term antibacterial experiments and compared it with $\mathtt{A g/}$ $\operatorname{Cu@CMCS}$ . The bacteria were co-cultured with the materials, and were supplemented every $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . As shown in Fig. 5a and b, at the dosage of MICs of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS, ${\sf A g/C u@C M C S}$ can completely inhibit bacterial growth within the first $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Nevertheless, at $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the inhi­ bition rates to E. coli and S. aureus decreased significantly, and at $36\mathrm{h}$ , ${\sf A g/C u@C M C S}$ could hardly inhibit bacterial reproduction. In contrast, the inhibition rates of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS remained above $94\%$ within $^{36\ \mathrm{h},}$ , indicating that the strategy of introducing MOFs into the metalcontained polysaccharide materials effectively improved the long-term antibacterial ability of the composite. Such antibacterial properties make the composite beads have the potential to be applied to water sterilization.
During the experiments, Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS beads could maintain their shape after antibacterial application due to the excellent support­ ing effect of the metal-crosslinked CMCS. Meanwhile, the SEM images showed that the MOF crystals profile on the composites were intact after once use, and only a few defects were found on the surfaces (Fig. 5f), indicating that the consumption of MOFs in the composite during the application process was low. We believe that Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS has the potential for multiple applications, and the key to restricting the sec­ ondary application was the loss of a large number of metal ions on CMCS in the material. Therefore, we designed a regeneration method for $\mathtt{A g/}$ Cu-BTC@CMCS by soaking the composite beads after antibacterial application in silver ion and copper ion solution to replenish the metal ions bound to CMCS chains (Fig. 5c). The regenerative antibacterial ability of the material was evaluated subsequently. As shown in Fig. 5d, in the first three rounds of long-term antibacterial experiments, the composite materials can achieve inhibition rates of ${>}93~\%$ .
The crystallinities and morphological changes of the material after each round of application were investigated by XRD and SEM, as shown in Fig. 5e and f. As the number of regeneration cycles increased, the intensity of the X-ray diffraction peaks attributed to the MOF gradually decreased. It can also be observed from the SEM pictures that the MOF crystals in the material are still relatively clear after the first round, while in the second round the crystals began to collapse. After four cy­ cles it is difficult to find octahedral crystals on the surface of the mate­ rial. This indicates that the decrease of the sustained bacteriostatic ability of the material after the third cycle was mainly attributed to the depletion of the MOF components. In addition, with the increase in the number of regeneration rounds, the intensity of the diffraction peaks at $2\Theta=38.1^{\circ}$ and $44.3^{\circ}$ in the XRD pattern of the material gradually increased, which can be attributed to the (111) and (200) lattice planes of elemental silver. This shows that a part of silver ions is converted into silver element in the recycling process, which is also one of the possible factors that lead to the reduction of the antibacterial ability of the ma­ terial. After repeated regeneration, the color of the material gradually turns green and shows a slight metallic luster (Fig. S4), suggesting that the strategy of repeatedly combining metal ions with polymer chains also has a limit on the cycle number.
## 3.4. Cytotoxicity of materials
Cell compatibility is a crucial problem of silver-containing materials. Therefore, the cytotoxicity of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS and Ag/Cu-BTC at their antibacterial concentrations was studied. HeLa cells were used as a model. The cells were mixed with the materials and incubated for $24\mathrm{h}$ , then the cell survival rates were determined by MTT colorimetry. As shown in Fig. 6, the cell survival rates of MOFs and composite materials decreased in varying degrees compared with the control group, indi­ cating that the materials have certain cytotoxicity. Among them, when the concentration of Ag/Cu-BTC was increased, the cell survival rate decreased rapidly. Relatively, Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS still has a higher cell survival rate $(87.30~\%)$ at its antibacterial concentration, which is an acceptable value in the field of biology. The results indicate that the bimetallic composite has limited cytotoxicity to cells and thus has high safety in the field of public health.
## 3.5. Assessment of swimming pool water disinfection
Given the excellent antibacterial ability and high biosafety of Ag/CuBTC@CMCS beads, the simulated swimming pool water were chosen as the model to verify the application potential. The experimental scheme is shown in Fig. 7a. At the beginning and the 24th hour in a cycle of test, a drug mixture equivalent to 20 people/26500 L/day bather insult was added to the water to simulate the water quality of an actual swimming pool, and bacteria were added at the same time. The composite beads were put into a gauze bag and immersed in the simulated swimming pool water, and the pool water was given proper flow. Then the changes in the colony forming units (CFU) of bacteria in the water were inves­ tigated. The initial bacterial concentration of the pool water was set to $1000\mathrm{\CFU/mL}$ . As shown in Fig. 7b, when no antibacterial agent is added, the bacteria can reproduce easily in the simulated swimming pool water, and the number of bacteria reaches the maximum value of about $4\times10^{4}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ at the 24th hour. This can be attributed to the sufficient nutrients contained in pool water due to the pollutants excreted by swimmers. With the use of the composite beads as an anti­ bacterial agent, the number of bacteria in the swimming pool water was significantly reduced. When the dosage of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS was increased to ${}_{2}{\bf\Gamma}_{\mathrm{g/L}}$ , the total number of colonies was controlled within $120~\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ in $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , which meets the requirement of the Chinese na­ tional standard GB 37488–2019.
Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS beads were recovered after the application and regenerated according to the strategy as the foregoing, and after that, the recycling experiments were carried out. As shown in Fig. 7c, the total bacteriostatic rate at the end of each application cycle remained above $97\%$ in the first four cycles. The concentration of metal ions in the pool water was detected when the experiment was carried out for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ and 48 h. As shown in Fig. 7d, in the early stage of application, the concen­ tration of ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ in water can quickly rise to a high level, thereby inhibiting the reproduction of bacteria. At the end of every application cycle, ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ concentrations dropped below $1.5\mathrm{ppm}$ and 5 ppm, respectively, because the dead bacteria would continue to consume the metal ions (Cao et al., 2020), which also prevents further improvement of the maximum effective duration. In addition, the adsorption of CMCS also limits the increase of metal ion concentration. However, the lower concentration of heavy metal ions also means a lower threat to human health. The safe values for human intake of silver ions and copper ions set by the World Health Organization are $5~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}/{\upk\mathrm{g}}$ and $0.5~\mathrm{mg/kg}$ body weight per day (Juling et al., 2018; Olivares & Uauy, 1996). People in such swimming pool water would not ingest enough heavy metals to endanger their health.
The performance of this material as a sustained-release antibacterial agent in simulated swimming pool was compared with the common commercial fungicides NaClO and trichloroisocyanuric acid (TCAA). As shown in Figs. S5–S7, Among the $48\mathrm{-h}$ test, the minimum number of feeding times for NaClO and TCAA is 7 and 5, respectively. In contrast, Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS beads only need to be added once at the beginning to inhibit bacterial reproduction in the whole process. However, due to the accumulation of nutrients and bacterial corpses, we can infer that it is more difficult to achieve the antibacterial goals in the experimental water than in the actual swimming pool water. Nevertheless, this can better illustrate the advantages of Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS beads in anti­ bacterial duration and required dosing frequency.
In addition, the mass loss of the material after use was investigated, and the number of composite beads with complete shapes before and after use was counted. As shown in Fig. 7e, after five times of regener­ ation, Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS still has nearly $80~\%$ of its mass remaining, and the shape retention rate reaches $91.80\%$ , which is beneficial for the practical application of reusable material. The above results show that Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS beads as an antibacterial agent can effectively inhibit the reproduction of bacteria in swimming pool water, and have good regeneration ability.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we successfully fabricated bimetallic Ag/CuBTC@CMCS beads as novel metal ion “warehouses” by the hybridiza­ tion of CMCS and Ag/Cu-MOFs, which overcame the difficulties of metal ions storage and controlled release. This safety, renewable and recy­ clability metal ion “warehouses” exhibited excellent performance of rapid sterilization and long-term antibacterial effects in water treat­ ment. The rapidly released metal ions from CMCS chains make the composite beads achieve nearly $99\%$ bacteria killing rate in $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , while the MOF crystals can provide long-term antibacterial performances by the sustained release of metal ions. More importantly, the strong in­ teractions between metal ions and CMCS in this “warehouses”, effec­ tively avoided possible risks of biosafety introduced by excessive or uncontrolled release of metal ions. It was noteworthy that Ag/CuBTC@CMCS beads could be recycled and regenerated repeatedly by replenishing the metal ions bound to polymer chains. In addition, the composite beads showed a good sustained bacteriostatic effect (bacte­ riostatic validity beyond $48~\mathrm{h}\mathrm{\i}$ , and had a unique advantage in feeding times compared with chlorine-containing fungicides in the simulated swimming pool water disinfection. Given the excellent antibacterial ability, renewability, and high biosafety, Ag/Cu-BTC@CMCS endows great potential as a water disinfection material. Our design of bimetallic composite provides a new idea for the storage and controlled release of metal ion and the development of efficient and safe water sterilization agents.
